[08:25.27]Passage One
[08:27.24]Although animal play is hard to identify because of its subjective nature, scientists have observed play behaviors in many species, including insects.
[08:38.69]The criteria that researchers use to identify play are whether the behavior is purposeless, if it occurs without external rewards, if it differs from more needs-based behaviors, if it happens repeatedly, and if the player is relaxed when it takes place. 
[08:56.55]Another clue that signals animals are at play is that humans and animals use the same part of their brains when engaging in these behaviors.
[09:06.08]Play helps prepare individuals to deal with life's challenges, tests boundaries, helps regulate emotions, and improves problem-solving skills, innovation, cooperation, and impulse control.
[09:20.58]Studies have even shown play helps release dopamine into an animal's brain.
[09:25.88]Another study discovered that birds who engage in play have a larger relative brain mass and live longer than birds who don't play. 
[09:34.78]Birds who didn't play at all had the smallest brain size, birds who played by themselves had slightly larger brains, birds who played with objects had larger brains, and birds who played with others had the largest relative brain size. 
[09:50.41]Interestingly, brain mass was not found to be associated with tool use.
[09:56.01]Animals are complex creatures who laugh and enjoy playing games simply because they're fun.
[10:02.55]Instead of letting our egos feel hurt that we share these similarities with our animal friends, maybe we can celebrate that they can also experience the joys of life.
