[11:36.21]The other kind of person wants to know how to eat more vegetables. 
[11:40.78]Thomas' simple answer to both groups is this: The best vegetables are the ones you actually want to eat. 
[11:47.72]As she puts it,¡°It doesn't matter how you cook them, because if they're going in the trash, they're not in your body.¡±
[11:54.33]However, research shows cooking methods do affect nutrition. 
[11:58.30]The first question to answer is whether raw vegetables are always healthier than cooked ones. 
[12:04.14]The answer is¡°Not always.¡± 
[12:06.49]It is true that many nutrients will be lost if vegetables are cooked with high heat for a long period of time. 
[12:12.57]But cooking can also make it easier for the body to digest. 
[12:16.66]Cooking over boiling water, or steaming, and in a microwave are considered the most nutritious methods. 
[12:23.51]These two methods use little time and require no fat. 
[12:26.93]Another healthy method is blanching. 
[12:29.65]This is when you boil vegetables in water for a minute or two, but not too long. 
[12:34.50]Dietitians also say that there is no problem using a small amount of fat. 
[12:39.00]Besides making vegetables taste better, a little cooking oil helps the body to absorb fat-soluble vitamins. 
