[14:39.29]Universities are blaming a¡°societal shift¡±for the cutting of dozens of foreign language degrees and even entire departments, citing a lack of demand among students. 
[14:51.06]But can years of study be easily replaced by AI or online translation tools£¿ 
[14:56.92]Not so, according to Michael Lynas, the UK country director for the Duolingo language app, who argues there is no good substitute for the hard graft of learning a language as a way of seeing another country's culture from the inside.
[15:13.35]Sometimes people say,¡°There are translation apps now so why would you need to learn a language£¿¡±
[15:20.10]But that's not what young people in the UK are saying. 
[15:23.72]Data show that not only were UK users more likely to be learning multiple languages than those in other countries, but that those aged under 22 were the most prolific users. 
[15:35.95]Megan Bowler, a lecturer at Oxford, said, ¡°Graduate linguists are interdisciplinary thinkers who approach subtle differences with a sophisticated awareness of societal and historical context. These skills and intellectual values are now even more important in the age of AI¡ªthe closure of language programs may well be shortsighted.¡±
[16:00.46]Over the past five years, nearly 50 centers of French, German, Italian and Iberian studies have closed or shrunk, while another wave of proposed closures is on the way, including the courses currently under threat at Nottingham and at Leicester University. 
[16:19.83]Nottingham said a ¡°societal shift¡±was behind the fall in applications but that the pipeline of language students from England's schools had been leaking for decades. 
[16:30.08]In the past 30 years the numbers taking A-level French have plummeted from close to 30,000 to below 10,000, and in German from 10,000 to about 2,000 each year.
[16:44.17]Only Spanish has shown improvement, but that and small increases in other languages such as Chinese have not made up for the losses, as fewer GCSE students now continue on to study languages at A-level and then university. 
[17:01.47]A lecturer in Spanish at Leicester University once said,¡°The people who come to study foreign languages with us are quite globally minded, and part of what they want is a degree that's going to allow them to work internationally.¡±
